<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute bronchitis, likely viral, after an upper respiratory infection (J20.9)</content>. Temperature 37.5 °C, heart rate 86, respiratory rate 18, oxygen saturation 96 percent, and a clear chest make pneumonia unlikely. He passed the water swallow screen and has not choked with meals, so aspiration is also unlikely despite his dysphagia. A chest radiograph and antibiotics are not indicated. [S]<list><item>Supportive care: honey in warm drinks, saline nasal rinse, and rest.</item><item>Continue the acetaminophen solution at no more than 3,000 mg a day. Do not add combination cold or flu products, because most contain more acetaminophen.</item><item>Avoid codeine-containing cough syrups. Plain dextromethorphan at night is acceptable if the cough disturbs sleep.</item><item>Repeat a home COVID-19 antigen test in 48 hours. If it is positive, call the same day, because antiviral treatment would be offered at his age with cancer.</item><item>The cough may last two to three weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Squamous cell carcinoma of the right tonsillar pillar, staging in progress (C09.1)</content>. The cough fits a cold and is not a sign of spread to the lungs, which the staging scan will assess. A respiratory infection can cause inflammatory uptake in the chest and neck nodes on PET/CT. [S]<list><item>Keep the staging PET/CT as booked. Message the head and neck team and radiology about the current cold so any reactive uptake is read in context.</item><item>Treatment-planning visit next week as scheduled.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nicotine dependence, cigars, two weeks abstinent (F17.290)</content>. Abstinent on varenicline. Cravings are stronger while he is ill and worried, a common relapse trigger. Coughing can increase for a few weeks after stopping smoking. [S]<list><item>Continue varenicline 1 mg twice daily after meals.</item><item>Brief counseling today: plan for cravings during illness and while waiting for results, and remove remaining cigars from the house.</item><item>Offer the state quitline for extra support between visits.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Oropharyngeal dysphagia from the tonsillar tumor (R13.12)</content>. Swallowing is unchanged; weight 76.5 kg, down 0.5 kg in a week. [S]<list><item>Continue soft, calorie-dense foods and two supplement drinks a day.</item><item>Keep the 60-minute wait after the lidocaine rinse before eating or drinking, because a numbed throat raises the risk of choking.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Guidelines Insights: Head and Neck Cancers, Version 2.2025 (2025); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in three days. Call or return the same day for a temperature above 38 °C, breathlessness, coughing or choking with swallowing, sputum or blood, or bleeding from the biopsy site. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-04: primary care visit after staging to coordinate nutrition, dental care, tobacco cessation, and symptom control before treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
